WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
A ninety-percent gain or a ten-percent miss?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Wait. They may describe the same proportion.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
A 1988 study examined attribute framing.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Complementary descriptions may shift evaluation.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
This differs from risky-choice framing.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Here are one hundred illustrative dots.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Ninety gains. Ten misses.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Change the wording. Keep the counts fixed.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Check the denominator, rules and costs.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
The feeling can change while the count stays.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Different conditions may make them unequal.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
One choice cannot rank your rationality.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Translate advertising into comparable outcomes.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Write both what is gained and what is lost.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Ask which group a statistic describes.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Wording lights one side. Turn it around.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
One translation step can improve comparison.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Place the choices against the same measure.
